# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Copy to `.env` (or `.env.local`) only if you need to change something.
#  With no .env at all, everything works: the bundled WebSocket relay is used, and QR codes point
#  at your computer's LAN address so a phone on the same Wi-Fi can open the remote.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Real-time pairing provider (build-time, read by Vite) ────────────────────────────────────
#   ws        → the bundled Node WebSocket relay (server/relay.js). Default. Needs `npm start`
#               or `npm run dev`, i.e. a host that can run Node + WebSockets.
#   supabase  → Supabase Realtime (Broadcast + Presence). For static hosting (Vercel, Netlify…).
VITE_REALTIME_PROVIDER=ws

# Only if the relay lives on a different host than the site:
# VITE_WS_URL=wss://relay.example.com/rc

# Base URL encoded into the pairing QR code. Leave empty to auto-detect
# (LAN IP in development, the page's own origin in production).
# VITE_PUBLIC_URL=https://averylane.example

# ── Supabase (only when VITE_REALTIME_PROVIDER=supabase) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Project Settings → API → "Project URL" and the "anon public" key. No tables are needed:
# pairing uses Realtime Broadcast + Presence on channels named `tv:<CODE>`.
# Make sure Realtime is enabled for the project.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-public-key

# ── Production server (`npm start`, runtime) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT=3000
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# Canonical public URL for QR codes, e.g. behind a reverse proxy:
# PUBLIC_URL=https://averylane.example
# Restrict which sites may open the relay socket (comma-separated):
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://averylane.example
# Behind a reverse proxy (Render, Fly, Nginx…), use X-Forwarded-For for per-IP limits:
# TRUST_PROXY=1
